  "live_records": {
    "q-213495": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213495",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A=\\{-2,1,5\\}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $-2$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $5$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $0$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:  \n\n|  | $-2$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n| $5$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(-2,5)$ and $(5,1).$ Since $R$ must be symmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(5,-2)\\in R,\\,(1,5)\\in R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all equal $1.$\n\n|  | $-2$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R⟹(y,x)\\notin R.$ Therefore, $(1,-2)\\notin R$ implies $(-2,1)\\notin R.$\n\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(-2,-2)$ is an element of $R.$\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $-2$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $?$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
