    "q-213603": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213603",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following relations are antisymmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ defined on ${R}^{+}$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $N$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x+2y\\ge 0,$ defined on $R$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(2,\\frac{1}{2})\\in R$ since $\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{(2)},$ and\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},2)\\in R$ since $2=\\frac{1}{(1/2)}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ and $(y,x)\\in S,$ then $x=y.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(2,1)\\in T$ since $2+2(1)\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $(1,2)\\in T$ since $1+2(2)\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
