    "q-214217": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214217",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274628/assets/630e6f7d0da56b1960dd7c6830eb6cfd18e22d039037bb3e0550edb38dfe8e89.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation defined on the set $A=\\{a,b,c\\}$ that is both symmetric and antisymmetric?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274628/assets/9d49cbf22786926c2b89802af855995c1d7e40c4385bdec4cc7a2a0dc0c65d72.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the point $(b,c)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(c,b)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,b)$ and $(b,a).$\n\n- Plot II represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents a relation that is both symmetric and antisymmetric. Notice that it has no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y.$ As a result, the propositions in both implications above are false. Hence, the implications themselves must be true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
