    "q-214218": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214218",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274628/assets/745736ba7890207c094433ab4ef133fc98a06e40e8b3abeed9143d4c52c90b16.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\},$ that is both symmetric and antisymmetric?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a relation that is both symmetric and antisymmetric. Notice that it has no pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y.$ As a result, the propositions in both implications above are false. Hence, the implications themselves must be true.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
